WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.101
Welcome to SteadierMind.

2
00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:08.713
Anxiety can affect both your thoughts and your body.

3
00:00:09.313 --> 00:00:13.846
One way to understand anxiety is to think of an alarm.

4
00:00:14.446 --> 00:00:20.752
When you anticipate a possible threat or challenge, you may notice worry, a

5
00:00:20.752 --> 00:00:23.662
faster heartbeat, sweating, or tense muscles.

6
00:00:24.262 --> 00:00:27.480
Imagine you’re preparing for a presentation.

7
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.401
Your heart races, and you worry about forgetting your words.

8
00:00:33.001 --> 00:00:37.569
Those sensations may reflect your response to the challenge.

9
00:00:38.169 --> 00:00:41.716
They don’t predict how the presentation will go.

10
00:00:42.316 --> 00:00:48.319
If it feels comfortable, notice one thing you can see, or your feet resting

11
00:00:48.319 --> 00:00:49.605
against the floor.

12
00:00:50.205 --> 00:00:52.634
You can skip this practice.

13
00:00:53.234 --> 00:00:58.927
There’s no need to force a deep breath or make every anxious feeling

14
00:00:58.927 --> 00:00:59.365
disappear.

15
00:00:59.965 --> 00:01:05.840
A small next step might be checking your notes, taking a brief pause, or

16
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.099
asking for support.

17
00:01:07.699 --> 00:01:11.901
Physical symptoms can have causes other than anxiety.

18
00:01:12.501 --> 00:01:17.719
Seek appropriate medical advice for new or concerning symptoms.

19
00:01:18.319 --> 00:01:24.646
If anxiety regularly affects your daily life, consider talking with a

20
00:01:24.646 --> 00:01:25.796
qualified professional.

21
00:01:26.396 --> 00:01:33.613
SteadierMind provides general wellness education, not diagnosis or treatment.
